1
00:00:25,792 --> 00:00:28,091
♪♪

2
00:00:36,336 --> 00:00:38,271
♪♪

3
00:00:49,316 --> 00:00:52,309
♪♪

4
00:01:15,409 --> 00:01:17,401
♪♪

5
00:01:50,611 --> 00:01:52,603
♪♪

6
00:02:20,574 --> 00:02:21,551
Mr. Larson.

7
00:02:21,575 --> 00:02:23,753
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

8
00:02:23,777 --> 00:02:26,589
As soon as the judge heard that,

9
00:02:26,613 --> 00:02:28,958
he called a recess, and
took us into his chambers,

10
00:02:28,982 --> 00:02:31,194
and he lit into the D.A.
like there was no tomorrow.

11
00:02:31,218 --> 00:02:32,428
I mean,

12
00:02:32,452 --> 00:02:36,199
all the poor guy could
do was move to dismiss.

13
00:02:36,223 --> 00:02:37,900
Why do I even bother?

14
00:02:37,924 --> 00:02:39,769
With what?

15
00:02:39,793 --> 00:02:41,804
Going to law school.

16
00:02:41,828 --> 00:02:44,607
When I can learn everything
I need to know from you.

17
00:02:44,631 --> 00:02:49,746
Well, there's no substitute
for experience, Andrea.

18
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:52,103
Yeah, you've
taught me that, too.

19
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:57,120
Oh, did you have a
chance to talk to Larry Klein

20
00:02:57,144 --> 00:02:58,554
about my clerking with
his firm this summer?

21
00:02:58,578 --> 00:03:00,990
No, I didn't.

22
00:03:01,014 --> 00:03:02,558
But you will?

23
00:03:02,582 --> 00:03:03,926
Of course.

24
00:03:03,950 --> 00:03:05,261
Next time I see him.

25
00:03:05,285 --> 00:03:06,285
Promise.

26
00:03:08,288 --> 00:03:11,934
So when is the next
time I'm going to see you?

27
00:03:11,958 --> 00:03:13,369
Is tomorrow soon enough?

28
00:03:13,393 --> 00:03:14,470
How about tonight?

29
00:03:14,494 --> 00:03:16,272
I got to work late.

30
00:03:16,296 --> 00:03:17,440
Oh.

31
00:03:17,464 --> 00:03:19,809
Tell you what, why don't we meet

32
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:22,045
at the apartment
tomorrow and have lunch?

33
00:03:22,069 --> 00:03:24,247
Better yet, we could
meet at the apartment

34
00:03:24,271 --> 00:03:25,581
and not have lunch.

35
00:03:34,881 --> 00:03:37,160
Um, got to get back.

36
00:03:37,184 --> 00:03:39,195
Tomorrow, 12:30?

37
00:03:39,219 --> 00:03:41,211
Can't wait.

38
00:04:09,216 --> 00:04:12,462
Oh, uh, M-Mr. Larson.

39
00:04:12,486 --> 00:04:13,963
Here are your messages.

40
00:04:13,987 --> 00:04:16,899
And Mr. Jordan is waiting.

41
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:17,900
Ed Jordan?

42
00:04:17,924 --> 00:04:19,535
What's he doing here?

43
00:04:19,559 --> 00:04:21,637
He had a 2:00 appointment.

44
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:23,706
Why in the hell
didn't you tell me?!

45
00:04:23,730 --> 00:04:25,908
I told you this morning.

46
00:04:25,932 --> 00:04:28,644
Your job isn't just to tell me,
it's to make sure that I know.

47
00:04:28,668 --> 00:04:30,847
Ed Jordan is important.

48
00:04:30,871 --> 00:04:32,849
You've embarrassed me, Phyllis.

49
00:04:32,873 --> 00:04:34,650
Mr. Larson...

50
00:04:34,674 --> 00:04:38,421
And the next time
that it happens, that's it.

51
00:04:38,445 --> 00:04:40,311
And I mean it.

52
00:04:42,315 --> 00:04:44,026
Ed, I'm terribly sorry.

53
00:04:44,050 --> 00:04:46,429
My girl neglected to remind
me that you were coming in.

54
00:04:46,453 --> 00:04:50,500
Please, have a seat.

55
00:04:50,524 --> 00:04:52,516
Get a pen and write this down.

56
00:04:55,595 --> 00:05:00,777
Find two clean cups,
fill them with coffee

57
00:05:00,801 --> 00:05:02,645
and bring them to us.

58
00:05:02,669 --> 00:05:04,661
Did you get that, dear?

59
00:05:09,309 --> 00:05:11,574
Yes, sir.

60
00:05:18,819 --> 00:05:21,397
Phyllis, for what it's worth,

61
00:05:21,421 --> 00:05:23,766
I heard you tell
him about that 2:00.

62
00:05:23,790 --> 00:05:27,637
You'd think, after all these
years, that I'd get used to it.

63
00:05:27,661 --> 00:05:29,238
He seems to get worse and worse.

64
00:05:29,262 --> 00:05:30,373
I know.

65
00:05:30,397 --> 00:05:31,908
Well, you know he's
not gonna fire you.

66
00:05:31,932 --> 00:05:33,142
Oh, no.

67
00:05:33,166 --> 00:05:36,379
He enjoys humiliating
me too much.

68
00:05:36,403 --> 00:05:39,549
I can't quit because, at my
age, who would hire me?

69
00:05:39,573 --> 00:05:41,384
Trapped like a rat in a cage.

70
00:05:41,408 --> 00:05:42,919
Oh, what can you do?

71
00:05:42,943 --> 00:05:46,022
I don't know.

72
00:05:46,046 --> 00:05:48,057
It's a terrible thing to say,

73
00:05:48,081 --> 00:05:51,279
but sometimes I
wish he were dead.

74
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:22,191
Oh, Frank.

75
00:06:25,785 --> 00:06:27,096
Eh, it must be the vitamins.

76
00:06:29,389 --> 00:06:32,068
Listen, I'll call you
tomorrow, 2:00, 2:30, and we

77
00:06:32,092 --> 00:06:33,302
can get together later, okay?

78
00:06:33,326 --> 00:06:34,604
Ride down with me?

79
00:06:35,762 --> 00:06:36,939
What do you got in mind?

80
00:06:36,963 --> 00:06:40,042
Well, we are 40 floors up.

81
00:06:55,415 --> 00:06:56,392
Andrea.

82
00:06:56,416 --> 00:06:57,927
What are you doing here?

83
00:06:57,951 --> 00:07:03,232
You were supposed to
be working late, remember?

84
00:07:03,256 --> 00:07:04,622
I brought you dinner.

85
00:07:06,626 --> 00:07:09,027
Who was that?

86
00:07:11,665 --> 00:07:15,124
Her name's Carol.

87
00:07:17,037 --> 00:07:18,748
How long has this been going on?

88
00:07:18,772 --> 00:07:19,749
It's none of your business.

89
00:07:19,773 --> 00:07:21,784
It's over with us, Andrea.

90
00:07:21,808 --> 00:07:23,319
The hell it is.

91
00:07:23,343 --> 00:07:24,754
You're a grown-up, Andrea.

92
00:07:24,778 --> 00:07:26,656
You knew you weren't the first.

93
00:07:27,914 --> 00:07:29,992
Surely you didn't
think you'd be the last.

94
00:07:30,016 --> 00:07:32,495
Nobody dumps me, Frank.

95
00:07:32,519 --> 00:07:33,829
Nobody!

96
00:07:33,853 --> 00:07:35,531
Look, I enjoyed
you, you enjoyed me.

97
00:07:35,555 --> 00:07:36,799
Now we both need a change.

98
00:07:36,823 --> 00:07:39,035
What about that job you
were setting up for me?

99
00:07:39,059 --> 00:07:40,970
I can hardly ask Larry Klein

100
00:07:40,994 --> 00:07:42,872
to hire a girl I
used to sleep with.

101
00:07:42,896 --> 00:07:43,873
Used to sleep with?!

102
00:07:43,897 --> 00:07:45,207
Yes!

103
00:07:45,231 --> 00:07:46,876
Past tense!

104
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:48,377
Go away.

105
00:07:48,401 --> 00:07:49,767
I'm busy.

106
00:08:06,753 --> 00:08:10,566
Andrea?

107
00:08:10,590 --> 00:08:13,302
Is that you?

108
00:08:13,326 --> 00:08:15,771
Yeah, Mom, it's me.

109
00:08:15,795 --> 00:08:17,540
I ordered some pizza.

110
00:08:17,564 --> 00:08:19,275
There's plenty left.

111
00:08:19,299 --> 00:08:20,710
No, thanks.

112
00:08:20,734 --> 00:08:23,704
I thought you said you
wouldn't be back till after 10:00.

113
00:08:27,774 --> 00:08:31,320
I have to tell you something.

114
00:08:31,344 --> 00:08:32,937
What is it?

115
00:08:34,981 --> 00:08:38,627
Mom, I had an affair
with Frank Larson.

116
00:08:38,651 --> 00:08:41,897
My Frank Larson?

117
00:08:41,921 --> 00:08:44,400
Yes.

118
00:08:44,424 --> 00:08:46,916
How could you?!

119
00:08:47,994 --> 00:08:49,839
I know you hate him, Mom.

120
00:08:49,863 --> 00:08:52,341
It's just that he
was so nice to me.

121
00:08:52,365 --> 00:08:58,763
He... he called me
and he sent me flowers.

122
00:08:59,839 --> 00:09:02,551
He told me he loved me.

123
00:09:02,575 --> 00:09:05,511
He seduced you.

124
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:10,659
Oh, God.

125
00:09:10,683 --> 00:09:12,595
My poor baby.

126
00:09:12,619 --> 00:09:13,596
Why didn't you tell me?

127
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:17,133
I think I killed him.

128
00:09:17,157 --> 00:09:18,157
What?

129
00:09:21,461 --> 00:09:22,738
I killed him.

130
00:09:22,762 --> 00:09:23,873
What?!

131
00:09:23,897 --> 00:09:26,609
What ha... what happened?!

132
00:09:31,504 --> 00:09:34,483
I went to his office tonight,

133
00:09:34,507 --> 00:09:35,918
only he was there
with another woman.

134
00:09:35,942 --> 00:09:39,822
And after she left,
I confronted him.

135
00:09:39,846 --> 00:09:42,124
He laughed at me, Mom.

136
00:09:42,148 --> 00:09:45,327
He said that...
I wasn't his first

137
00:09:45,351 --> 00:09:46,629
and I wouldn't be his last.

138
00:09:46,653 --> 00:09:47,963
I was just so hurt,

139
00:09:47,987 --> 00:09:50,366
and I was so angry, I didn't
know what I was doing!

140
00:09:50,390 --> 00:09:53,402
I picked up a-a bookend

141
00:09:53,426 --> 00:09:56,739
and hit him over the head.

142
00:10:04,237 --> 00:10:06,229
But I...

143
00:10:09,476 --> 00:10:12,455
Did, uh, d-did anyone see you?

144
00:10:12,479 --> 00:10:14,107
No. No.

145
00:10:16,116 --> 00:10:19,829
How did you get
into the building?

146
00:10:19,853 --> 00:10:20,830
How'd you get in?

147
00:10:20,854 --> 00:10:23,847
I took your card.

148
00:10:26,459 --> 00:10:29,657
G-G-Give it to me.

149
00:10:32,632 --> 00:10:36,612
How, uh... What did you do?

150
00:10:36,636 --> 00:10:38,047
W-With the bookend.

151
00:10:38,071 --> 00:10:41,283
I-I threw it in a Dumpster
somewhere downtown.

152
00:10:41,307 --> 00:10:44,220
I-I didn't even realize
it was still in my hand

153
00:10:44,244 --> 00:10:46,839
until I was out at my car.

154
00:10:51,818 --> 00:10:53,395
If anyone asks you,

155
00:10:53,419 --> 00:10:57,199
you tell them that
you were here all night.

156
00:10:57,223 --> 00:10:58,634
You were studying.

157
00:10:58,658 --> 00:11:01,025
You hear me?

158
00:11:01,761 --> 00:11:03,320
Yeah.

159
00:11:05,298 --> 00:11:06,609
What are you gonna do?

160
00:11:06,633 --> 00:11:10,012
I'm going to make
sure that bastard's death

161
00:11:10,036 --> 00:11:12,505
doesn't ruin your life.

162
00:11:14,541 --> 00:11:16,976
You take a shower and go to bed.

163
00:11:47,006 --> 00:11:48,998
♪♪

164
00:12:18,538 --> 00:12:22,184
No, the, uh, janitor's
absolutely right.

165
00:12:22,208 --> 00:12:23,786
I was in the office last night.

166
00:12:23,810 --> 00:12:26,021
I just didn't kill Mr. Larson.

167
00:12:26,045 --> 00:12:28,657
What were you doing
there in the first place?

168
00:12:31,150 --> 00:12:35,030
Mr. Larson and I had
kind of a blowup yesterday.

169
00:12:35,054 --> 00:12:37,166
He embarrassed me in
front of the whole office.

170
00:12:37,190 --> 00:12:40,102
More or less
threatened to fire me.

171
00:12:40,126 --> 00:12:43,239
I went to the office last night
to try and smooth things over.

172
00:12:43,263 --> 00:12:44,707
I knew he was working late.

173
00:12:44,731 --> 00:12:46,308
When I got there,
his door was closed.

174
00:12:46,332 --> 00:12:47,509
He was talking to somebody.

175
00:12:47,533 --> 00:12:49,378
So I went out to my car.

176
00:12:49,402 --> 00:12:52,214
I-I read the paper.

177
00:12:52,238 --> 00:12:56,118
About 45 minutes later, I
went back to the office and...

178
00:12:56,142 --> 00:12:58,854
whoever he was
talking to was gone.

179
00:12:58,878 --> 00:12:59,989
And he was dead.

180
00:13:00,013 --> 00:13:03,659
I just turned around and I ran.

181
00:13:03,683 --> 00:13:06,028
I know I should've
called the police,

182
00:13:06,052 --> 00:13:08,817
but it was such a shock.

183
00:13:10,690 --> 00:13:14,303
Besides, I'd be lying if I
said that it didn't occur to me

184
00:13:14,327 --> 00:13:16,421
that I might be blamed.

185
00:13:18,264 --> 00:13:22,511
This person he was talking
to... Have any idea who it was?

186
00:13:22,535 --> 00:13:23,846
No.

187
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:26,048
- Man or woman?
- Man.

188
00:13:26,072 --> 00:13:29,952
If you heard his voice again,
you think you'd recognize it?

189
00:13:29,976 --> 00:13:31,706
I might.

190
00:13:33,413 --> 00:13:34,690
No.

191
00:13:34,714 --> 00:13:35,758
His voice was too muffled.

192
00:13:35,782 --> 00:13:36,759
Oh.

193
00:13:36,783 --> 00:13:38,115
Muffled.

194
00:13:40,119 --> 00:13:41,930
You worked for
him for a long time?

195
00:13:41,954 --> 00:13:44,066
22 years.

196
00:13:44,090 --> 00:13:45,090
Mm.

197
00:13:47,026 --> 00:13:48,892
You say he
threatened to fire you?

198
00:13:50,596 --> 00:13:54,363
Lately, he's been threatening
to find someone younger.

199
00:13:57,537 --> 00:13:59,715
Will you take my case?

200
00:14:01,841 --> 00:14:04,553
Well...

201
00:14:04,577 --> 00:14:08,757
Mr. Matlock, I did
not kill Frank Larson.

202
00:14:08,781 --> 00:14:12,274
That is the absolute
God's honest truth.

203
00:14:19,359 --> 00:14:22,386
Well, let's see if we
can get you out of here.

204
00:14:34,874 --> 00:14:35,918
Morning, morning.

205
00:14:37,243 --> 00:14:38,253
Bobby.

206
00:14:38,277 --> 00:14:39,722
Morning, Ben.

207
00:14:39,746 --> 00:14:41,690
So you took her case, huh?

208
00:14:41,714 --> 00:14:44,526
Yeah, she's a nice lady.

209
00:14:47,487 --> 00:14:49,131
Have you found
the murder weapon?

210
00:14:49,155 --> 00:14:50,532
Uh, no.

211
00:14:50,556 --> 00:14:52,167
But we know he was
struck over the head once

212
00:14:52,191 --> 00:14:54,002
with a heavy, flat-edged object.

213
00:14:54,026 --> 00:14:58,006
So we're assuming it's the
bookend that matches this.

214
00:14:58,030 --> 00:15:01,643
Killer must have taken
it with her when she fled.

215
00:15:01,667 --> 00:15:03,078
- With him.
- Hmm?

216
00:15:03,102 --> 00:15:04,480
With him. It was a man.

217
00:15:04,504 --> 00:15:06,615
How do you know that?

218
00:15:08,508 --> 00:15:09,485
Sure.

219
00:15:09,509 --> 00:15:13,856
Uh, my client heard Frank Larson

220
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:18,527
arguing with, uh,
somebody 45 minutes before,

221
00:15:18,551 --> 00:15:22,331
before she, uh.
She found the body.

222
00:15:22,355 --> 00:15:26,034
And, uh, um, said it was a man.

223
00:15:26,058 --> 00:15:27,058
Right.

224
00:15:29,429 --> 00:15:31,106
I don't suppose
you've run into anybody

225
00:15:31,130 --> 00:15:32,341
could give us an
idea who it was?

226
00:15:32,365 --> 00:15:33,675
No.

227
00:15:33,699 --> 00:15:35,511
But if you're gonna
look, you better do it fast.

228
00:15:35,535 --> 00:15:37,646
Frank Larson's widow wants
all of his personal effects

229
00:15:37,670 --> 00:15:39,615
cleared out of here
as soon as possible.

230
00:15:39,639 --> 00:15:41,904
Oh, in a hurry, huh?

231
00:15:56,556 --> 00:15:58,434
- Easy.
- Okay.

232
00:15:58,458 --> 00:16:00,369
Mrs. Larson.

233
00:16:00,393 --> 00:16:03,205
Uh... uh, hi.

234
00:16:03,229 --> 00:16:04,606
- Watch it.
- Hi. I'm Ben Matlock.

235
00:16:04,630 --> 00:16:06,141
I represent Phyllis Todd.

236
00:16:06,165 --> 00:16:08,544
Hello. The matching
end tables go, too.

237
00:16:08,568 --> 00:16:09,845
Everything in the study goes.

238
00:16:09,869 --> 00:16:10,846
Yes, ma'am.

239
00:16:10,870 --> 00:16:11,847
Are you moving?

240
00:16:11,871 --> 00:16:13,048
No. No.

241
00:16:13,072 --> 00:16:14,650
I'm just getting
rid of a few things

242
00:16:14,674 --> 00:16:16,785
- I have no use for anymore.
- Oh.

243
00:16:16,809 --> 00:16:17,886
Whoa.

244
00:16:17,910 --> 00:16:20,956
What a nice desk!

245
00:16:20,980 --> 00:16:23,792
It was my husband's.

246
00:16:23,816 --> 00:16:26,995
Oh, you're getting rid of
all your husband's things?

247
00:16:27,019 --> 00:16:28,464
Well, he's dead, isn't he?

248
00:16:28,488 --> 00:16:30,332
Yeah.

249
00:16:30,356 --> 00:16:34,225
Yeah, he... he's been that way
for close to a whole day now.

250
00:16:35,261 --> 00:16:36,271
Yes.

251
00:16:36,295 --> 00:16:38,907
Uh, I guess he has.

252
00:16:38,931 --> 00:16:41,443
You, uh, you didn't get along?

253
00:16:41,467 --> 00:16:42,678
Oh, no, no.

254
00:16:42,702 --> 00:16:44,446
We got along just fine.

255
00:16:44,470 --> 00:16:46,915
He had his affairs, I had mine.

256
00:16:46,939 --> 00:16:48,450
Oh.

257
00:16:48,474 --> 00:16:52,955
Were they b-business, uh,
or-or-or pleasure af-affairs?

258
00:16:52,979 --> 00:16:54,823
Both.

259
00:16:54,847 --> 00:16:56,625
Oh.

260
00:16:56,649 --> 00:16:59,995
Was he seeing, um,
somebody recently?

261
00:17:00,019 --> 00:17:02,965
He was always seeing
somebody recently.

262
00:17:02,989 --> 00:17:05,267
Would you have any
idea who they were?

263
00:17:05,291 --> 00:17:07,503
I couldn't have cared less.

264
00:17:07,527 --> 00:17:09,805
Did you know your husband

265
00:17:09,829 --> 00:17:11,907
was gonna be
working late last night?

266
00:17:12,932 --> 00:17:14,943
Oh, sure.

267
00:17:14,967 --> 00:17:17,379
Did you do anything special?

268
00:17:17,403 --> 00:17:20,015
Yes, I went to the
East Side Athletic Club,

269
00:17:20,039 --> 00:17:22,251
played tennis with
some friends, had dinner.

270
00:17:22,275 --> 00:17:25,420
And you were there
till... Well, let's see.

271
00:17:25,444 --> 00:17:27,556
We got there about 6:00.

272
00:17:27,580 --> 00:17:29,558
Uh, we were there
till about 10:00.

273
00:17:31,217 --> 00:17:33,061
Sorry, ma'am.

274
00:17:33,085 --> 00:17:34,229
No problem.

275
00:17:34,253 --> 00:17:36,965
Those are Frank's golf trophies.

276
00:17:36,989 --> 00:17:39,268
Most, if not all of them,
completely undeserved.

277
00:17:39,292 --> 00:17:40,269
Oh.

278
00:17:40,293 --> 00:17:42,504
He cheated at that, too.

279
00:17:42,528 --> 00:17:43,552
Oh.

280
00:17:51,137 --> 00:17:52,114
Hey, Michelle.

281
00:17:52,138 --> 00:17:53,615
- Hey.
- Is Ben here?

282
00:17:53,639 --> 00:17:56,351
He just left to go see Phyllis.

283
00:17:56,375 --> 00:17:57,619
Check out the athletic club?

284
00:17:57,643 --> 00:18:00,155
Yeah. Laura Larson was
there from 6:00 to 10:00

285
00:18:00,179 --> 00:18:01,156
just like she said.

286
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:02,457
Witnesses all over the place.

287
00:18:02,481 --> 00:18:04,693
Hmm. Well, I didn't get very far

288
00:18:04,717 --> 00:18:06,962
with Phyllis's coworkers either.

289
00:18:06,986 --> 00:18:09,197
Everybody loves her, hated him,

290
00:18:09,221 --> 00:18:11,033
and have no idea
who he could've been

291
00:18:11,057 --> 00:18:12,935
talking to in his
office last night.

292
00:18:12,959 --> 00:18:15,604
This guy Larson, he kills me.

293
00:18:15,628 --> 00:18:18,273
I can understand a guy wanting
to have a fling now and then.

294
00:18:18,297 --> 00:18:20,442
But, I mean, Larson
wanted to have them all.

295
00:18:20,466 --> 00:18:22,544
Well, it's a two-way street.

296
00:18:22,568 --> 00:18:24,400
He did get a little
help, you know.

297
00:18:29,041 --> 00:18:30,752
From who?

298
00:18:30,776 --> 00:18:33,355
From women.

299
00:18:33,379 --> 00:18:35,290
Oh, he wouldn't have
been nearly so successful

300
00:18:35,314 --> 00:18:36,792
in all his so-called conquests

301
00:18:36,816 --> 00:18:38,994
if he didn't get a
signal from women.

302
00:18:39,018 --> 00:18:40,629
Uh, what kind of signal?

303
00:18:40,653 --> 00:18:43,632
The three A's.

304
00:18:43,656 --> 00:18:47,603
I'm approachable, I'm
agreeable, I'm available.

305
00:18:47,627 --> 00:18:50,038
A woman can have just
about any man she wants

306
00:18:50,062 --> 00:18:52,641
when she sends
out those signals.

307
00:18:52,665 --> 00:18:54,099
Oh...

308
00:18:56,102 --> 00:18:57,346
Any man?

309
00:18:57,370 --> 00:19:00,315
Yeah, it happens.

310
00:19:00,339 --> 00:19:02,317
Even, say, someone like Ben?

311
00:19:04,076 --> 00:19:05,187
Oh, Conrad.

312
00:19:05,211 --> 00:19:07,089
Exactly.

313
00:19:07,113 --> 00:19:09,091
You make goo-goo eyes at him,

314
00:19:09,115 --> 00:19:11,093
you wiggle your
little antenna at him,

315
00:19:11,117 --> 00:19:12,494
and he won't even notice it.

316
00:19:12,518 --> 00:19:14,129
He'll probably start
shining his shoes

317
00:19:14,153 --> 00:19:15,697
or scratch his back
up against the wall.

318
00:19:15,721 --> 00:19:17,699
Because he won't fall for it.

319
00:19:17,723 --> 00:19:20,969
I guarantee you
he won't fall for it.

320
00:19:20,993 --> 00:19:22,985
I think you're wrong, Conrad.

321
00:19:26,899 --> 00:19:28,076
Then do it.

322
00:19:28,100 --> 00:19:29,077
What?

323
00:19:29,101 --> 00:19:30,145
You do it.

324
00:19:30,169 --> 00:19:31,279
Give Ben the signal.

325
00:19:31,303 --> 00:19:34,102
I got 50 bucks that
says it won't work.

326
00:19:36,208 --> 00:19:38,920
Unless of course you
don't think you can do it.

327
00:19:38,944 --> 00:19:40,071
Yeah.

328
00:19:42,114 --> 00:19:43,225
Oh, I'm tempted.

329
00:19:43,249 --> 00:19:45,961
Ooh, then try it.

330
00:19:45,985 --> 00:19:49,387
You got the signals,
but I know Ben Matlock.

331
00:19:51,424 --> 00:19:52,634
- You're on.
- Great!

332
00:19:52,658 --> 00:19:54,202
- 50 bucks.
- 50 bucks.

333
00:20:09,408 --> 00:20:10,585
- Hi.
- Oh.

334
00:20:10,609 --> 00:20:12,554
I hope you don't
mind my stopping by.

335
00:20:12,578 --> 00:20:13,789
I-I want to ask you something.

336
00:20:13,813 --> 00:20:15,023
Of course not. Come on in.

337
00:20:15,047 --> 00:20:16,391
You're sure I'm
not interrupting?

338
00:20:16,415 --> 00:20:17,726
No, not at all.

339
00:20:17,750 --> 00:20:19,161
Uh, Andrea.

340
00:20:19,185 --> 00:20:20,295
Come and meet my attorney.

341
00:20:20,319 --> 00:20:22,652
This is Ben Matlock.

342
00:20:24,557 --> 00:20:26,168
Oh.

343
00:20:26,192 --> 00:20:27,302
How do you do?

344
00:20:27,326 --> 00:20:29,905
Oh... I hear you're
in law school.

345
00:20:29,929 --> 00:20:31,406
Yes, I am.

346
00:20:31,430 --> 00:20:33,442
My daughter went to law school

347
00:20:33,466 --> 00:20:36,211
and practices in Philadelphia.

348
00:20:36,235 --> 00:20:38,814
If I sound like a proud
father, it's because I am.

349
00:20:39,839 --> 00:20:42,918
That's wonderful, Mr. Matlock.

350
00:20:42,942 --> 00:20:45,954
I hope my mother can
say the same someday.

351
00:20:45,978 --> 00:20:47,055
Oh, I'm sorry.

352
00:20:47,079 --> 00:20:48,156
Would you like some lemonade?

353
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:49,825
Oh, no, thank you.

354
00:20:49,849 --> 00:20:51,827
You wanted to ask me something.

355
00:20:51,851 --> 00:20:52,994
Uh, yeah, yeah.

356
00:20:53,018 --> 00:20:56,131
I had an opportunity
to-to speak with, uh,

357
00:20:56,155 --> 00:20:57,532
Frank Larson's widow.

358
00:20:57,556 --> 00:20:59,935
And apparently, he-he wasn't

359
00:20:59,959 --> 00:21:04,454
what-what you'd
call... faithful.

360
00:21:06,165 --> 00:21:07,943
That does not surprise me.

361
00:21:07,967 --> 00:21:10,645
I was hoping maybe
you could help me

362
00:21:10,669 --> 00:21:14,783
figure out what lady
or ladies he was seeing.

363
00:21:14,807 --> 00:21:17,886
You mean, you think he
might have been murdered

364
00:21:17,910 --> 00:21:19,187
by a jealous husband?

365
00:21:19,211 --> 00:21:21,289
Oh, could be, could be.

366
00:21:21,313 --> 00:21:23,625
A lot of women phoned.

367
00:21:23,649 --> 00:21:25,927
I never knew who they were.

368
00:21:25,951 --> 00:21:28,163
You might look
at the phone logs.

369
00:21:28,187 --> 00:21:30,198
They're in my
desk at the office.

370
00:21:30,222 --> 00:21:33,435
Oh, okay. I'll do that.
Thank you, thank you.

371
00:21:33,459 --> 00:21:34,536
That's it?

372
00:21:34,560 --> 00:21:35,537
Yeah.

373
00:21:35,561 --> 00:21:36,838
Yeah, that's it.

374
00:21:36,862 --> 00:21:39,541
E-Except I'm glad to
finally meet you, Andrea.

375
00:21:39,565 --> 00:21:41,376
Same here, Mr. Matlock.

376
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:43,278
And thank you for
taking my mother's case.

377
00:21:43,302 --> 00:21:44,346
Oh, it's a pleasure.

378
00:21:44,370 --> 00:21:48,984
And as-as one
legal mind to another,

379
00:21:49,008 --> 00:21:52,721
uh, is there anything
you remember

380
00:21:52,745 --> 00:21:56,558
about the night of the murder
that might help your mother?

381
00:21:56,582 --> 00:21:57,926
Oh.

382
00:21:57,950 --> 00:21:59,394
I wish I did.

383
00:21:59,418 --> 00:22:02,497
Um... I came home around 6:30.

384
00:22:02,521 --> 00:22:07,869
She left to talk to
Mr. Larson at around 7:30.

385
00:22:07,893 --> 00:22:11,139
I ordered a pizza,
studied and went to bed.

386
00:22:11,163 --> 00:22:12,507
- Ah.
- That's it.

387
00:22:12,531 --> 00:22:15,510
Okay. Thank you. Okay.

388
00:22:15,534 --> 00:22:19,403
Well, I'll-I'll, uh, be
talking with you. Bye.

389
00:22:42,561 --> 00:22:44,039
Hi, Michelle.

390
00:22:44,063 --> 00:22:46,908
Well?

391
00:22:46,932 --> 00:22:49,444
Well, I just went through
two years worth of phone logs.

392
00:22:49,468 --> 00:22:50,812
Mm-hmm? Find anything?

393
00:22:50,836 --> 00:22:52,180
Yeah.

394
00:22:52,204 --> 00:22:54,316
A pattern.

395
00:22:54,340 --> 00:22:55,717
I'm assuming that the women

396
00:22:55,741 --> 00:22:57,552
who called Frank
Larson most frequently

397
00:22:57,576 --> 00:22:59,287
were the women
he had affairs with,

398
00:22:59,311 --> 00:23:02,440
Shelly, Trish, Linda,
Melanie and Denise.

399
00:23:07,086 --> 00:23:10,265
How did he have
time to practice law?

400
00:23:10,289 --> 00:23:14,002
Well, maybe because
he saw one at a time.

401
00:23:14,026 --> 00:23:15,871
Now, now, see,

402
00:23:15,895 --> 00:23:19,407
Shelly called him every
day for about four months.

403
00:23:19,431 --> 00:23:21,877
And then her calls
stopped and Trish's began.

404
00:23:21,901 --> 00:23:23,478
She called him for
about four months,

405
00:23:23,502 --> 00:23:25,494
and then Linda's calls began.

406
00:23:28,007 --> 00:23:30,452
Anything wrong?

407
00:23:30,476 --> 00:23:33,088
No.

408
00:23:33,112 --> 00:23:34,422
Go ahead.

409
00:23:34,446 --> 00:23:36,057
Well, the really
interesting thing is,

410
00:23:36,081 --> 00:23:39,427
about four months ago,
Denise's calls stopped,

411
00:23:39,451 --> 00:23:41,830
but nobody else's began.

412
00:23:41,854 --> 00:23:44,966
Maybe he needed a rest.

413
00:23:44,990 --> 00:23:46,968
Well, Mrs. Larson
said she thought

414
00:23:46,992 --> 00:23:50,005
that he was cheating on her
right up till the night he died.

415
00:23:50,029 --> 00:23:51,473
Well, then he was.

416
00:23:51,497 --> 00:23:53,508
Wives always know.

417
00:23:53,532 --> 00:23:56,444
Maybe his last affair
never called the office.

418
00:23:56,468 --> 00:23:58,380
- Huh.
- But why?

419
00:23:58,404 --> 00:24:00,548
I don't know.

420
00:24:00,572 --> 00:24:04,202
Something has gotten in here.

421
00:24:07,446 --> 00:24:10,792
Oh, I know.

422
00:24:10,816 --> 00:24:13,342
The exterminator
was here yesterday.

423
00:24:14,420 --> 00:24:17,899
But the smell's
usually gone by now.

424
00:24:17,923 --> 00:24:20,068
Hmm.

425
00:24:20,092 --> 00:24:22,789
So, this was Larson's love nest.

426
00:24:24,830 --> 00:24:28,777
He didn't rent it for
the view, did he?

427
00:24:28,801 --> 00:24:30,445
I'll check the bedroom.

428
00:24:30,469 --> 00:24:32,314
Okay.

429
00:24:42,948 --> 00:24:45,008
Mm-hmm.

430
00:24:49,955 --> 00:24:51,099
Nothing.

431
00:24:51,123 --> 00:24:54,002
Nothing?

432
00:24:54,026 --> 00:24:56,928
He didn't have many
home-cooked meals, either.

433
00:24:58,063 --> 00:25:01,556
Ever heard of
D'Amato's restaurant?

434
00:25:02,701 --> 00:25:04,879
- No.
- Huh.

435
00:25:07,339 --> 00:25:09,851
Yeah. I knew Frank Larson.

436
00:25:09,875 --> 00:25:12,153
I mean, I knew who he was.

437
00:25:12,177 --> 00:25:14,022
He came in here all the time.

438
00:25:14,046 --> 00:25:16,691
In fact, he came in
here the day he died.

439
00:25:16,715 --> 00:25:19,327
Was he with anybody?

440
00:25:19,351 --> 00:25:20,662
Some blonde.

441
00:25:20,686 --> 00:25:22,797
He's been bringing her
in for a couple of months.

442
00:25:22,821 --> 00:25:25,533
We are talking
young and gorgeous.

443
00:25:25,557 --> 00:25:26,668
Did you get a name on her?

444
00:25:26,692 --> 00:25:29,204
No. But I did overhear her

445
00:25:29,228 --> 00:25:31,239
saying something
once that blew me away.

446
00:25:31,263 --> 00:25:32,574
Yeah? What was that?

447
00:25:32,598 --> 00:25:34,943
She was talking about
taking some criminal law class.

448
00:25:34,967 --> 00:25:36,911
I couldn't believe it.

449
00:25:36,935 --> 00:25:40,064
This beautiful chick
was going to law school.

450
00:25:43,709 --> 00:25:47,476
I had a huge dinner last
night. It was a turkey burger.

451
00:25:48,347 --> 00:25:50,525
Oh, hi. Glad you could make it.

452
00:25:50,549 --> 00:25:51,593
Want some coffee?

453
00:25:51,617 --> 00:25:52,861
Oh, no thanks.

454
00:25:52,885 --> 00:25:54,929
I've got to get back
to my study group.

455
00:25:54,953 --> 00:25:56,564
Oh.

456
00:25:56,588 --> 00:25:59,267
Is something wrong
with my mother?

457
00:25:59,291 --> 00:26:02,704
Oh, no. No. I just wanted
to give you something.

458
00:26:02,728 --> 00:26:05,407
<i>Black's Law Dictionary?</i>

459
00:26:05,431 --> 00:26:07,075
- You're giving this to me?
- Yeah.

460
00:26:07,099 --> 00:26:09,811
It was my daughter's and
she doesn't need it anymore,

461
00:26:09,835 --> 00:26:11,112
and I thought maybe
you could use it.

462
00:26:11,136 --> 00:26:13,782
Uh, yes, I can.

463
00:26:13,806 --> 00:26:16,284
- Thank you.
- Yeah, yeah.

464
00:26:16,308 --> 00:26:17,886
Well, I'd better get back.

465
00:26:17,910 --> 00:26:18,920
Okay.

466
00:26:18,944 --> 00:26:20,188
My best to your mother.

467
00:26:20,212 --> 00:26:23,425
Tell me, Mr. Matlock,
how's her case going?

468
00:26:23,449 --> 00:26:25,960
Um, it's fine.

469
00:26:25,984 --> 00:26:28,229
It's coming along.

470
00:26:28,253 --> 00:26:29,631
Good.

471
00:26:29,655 --> 00:26:31,599
- Yeah.
- Thanks again for the book.

472
00:26:31,623 --> 00:26:32,901
- Okay.
- Buh-bye.

473
00:26:32,925 --> 00:26:34,917
Bye.

474
00:26:43,235 --> 00:26:45,346
- Well?
- That's her.

475
00:26:45,370 --> 00:26:47,715
That's the blonde that
Frank Lawson was with.

476
00:26:47,739 --> 00:26:49,884
Something else, isn't she?

477
00:26:49,908 --> 00:26:51,968
Yeah, she sure is.

478
00:27:02,821 --> 00:27:03,932
Miss Hawkins?

479
00:27:03,956 --> 00:27:05,967
Miss Hawkins!

480
00:27:05,991 --> 00:27:07,135
Mm-hmm?

481
00:27:07,159 --> 00:27:08,937
- Will you get that?
- Uh-huh.

482
00:27:08,961 --> 00:27:10,827
Uh-huh?

483
00:27:14,066 --> 00:27:16,511
Oh, Miss Thomas, I
hardly recognize you.

484
00:27:16,535 --> 00:27:17,612
Shh, shh, shh.

485
00:27:17,636 --> 00:27:19,114
What have you done to yourself?

486
00:27:19,138 --> 00:27:20,915
Oh, nothing, really.

487
00:27:20,939 --> 00:27:23,284
It's probably my new hairdo.

488
00:27:23,308 --> 00:27:25,868
Well, you certainly look nice.

489
00:27:31,817 --> 00:27:33,094
Hi, Ben.

490
00:27:33,118 --> 00:27:34,596
Hi, Michelle.

491
00:27:34,620 --> 00:27:36,865
Here's a little bit of news
that will curl your eyebrows.

492
00:27:36,889 --> 00:27:40,702
Andrea was having an
affair with her mother's boss.

493
00:27:40,726 --> 00:27:42,437
- No.
- Yeah.

494
00:27:42,461 --> 00:27:45,140
Now, that puts a whole new
wrinkle on things, doesn't it?

495
00:27:45,164 --> 00:27:47,976
Yeah.

496
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,778
I guess that's why the
phone calls stopped.

497
00:27:50,802 --> 00:27:52,447
Yeah. She couldn't
call him at the office.

498
00:27:52,471 --> 00:27:53,882
Her mother would find out.

499
00:27:53,906 --> 00:27:55,717
Might explain
something else, too.

500
00:27:55,741 --> 00:27:56,918
What's that?

501
00:27:56,942 --> 00:27:58,686
Why he's dead.

502
00:27:58,710 --> 00:28:01,890
Her four months were up.

503
00:28:01,914 --> 00:28:03,382
What?

504
00:28:05,217 --> 00:28:06,594
Do that again.

505
00:28:06,618 --> 00:28:08,196
Do what again?

506
00:28:08,220 --> 00:28:10,131
That-That little walk.

507
00:28:10,155 --> 00:28:11,332
You mean up and down here?

508
00:28:11,356 --> 00:28:13,134
Up and down.

509
00:28:13,158 --> 00:28:14,869
You like-like this? Yeah.

510
00:28:16,662 --> 00:28:19,240
That's it. That's it.

511
00:28:19,264 --> 00:28:21,009
- That's it. That's it.
- What?

512
00:28:21,033 --> 00:28:22,310
- That's it.
- What?

513
00:28:22,334 --> 00:28:25,246
That's it. Ernie Ford...
Ernie Ford said a line

514
00:28:25,270 --> 00:28:27,282
to Lucille Ball on
a show one time.

515
00:28:27,306 --> 00:28:29,384
She was walking this
little wiggly walk like that.

516
00:28:29,408 --> 00:28:32,687
He said, "Ma'am, you got
a itch in your get-along."

517
00:28:37,416 --> 00:28:38,560
Whoo!

518
00:28:39,952 --> 00:28:42,387
You got an itch
in your get-along.

519
00:28:43,822 --> 00:28:45,800
Excuse me, Mr. Matlock.

520
00:28:45,824 --> 00:28:47,735
Would you like me
to fix you some lunch?

521
00:28:47,759 --> 00:28:50,872
Sure. Want some lunch?

522
00:28:50,896 --> 00:28:53,775
Uh, why don't we go out?

523
00:28:53,799 --> 00:28:56,578
You want to go out? Okay.
Thank you, Miss Hawkins, anyway.

524
00:28:56,602 --> 00:28:58,179
Where you want to go?

525
00:28:58,203 --> 00:29:00,682
Well, the weather is so nice.

526
00:29:00,706 --> 00:29:02,417
Why don't we eat alfresco?

527
00:29:02,441 --> 00:29:03,785
Al who?

528
00:29:03,809 --> 00:29:05,119
- Outdoors.
- Oh.

529
00:29:05,143 --> 00:29:07,589
It's so pleasant
and we can talk.

530
00:29:07,613 --> 00:29:09,591
Okay.

531
00:29:09,615 --> 00:29:12,227
I know just the place.

532
00:29:12,251 --> 00:29:15,244
Hey, taxi! Taxi!

533
00:29:17,656 --> 00:29:20,368
What a wonderful idea.

534
00:29:20,392 --> 00:29:21,769
The sidewalk cafe.

535
00:29:21,793 --> 00:29:23,438
What's it called?

536
00:29:23,462 --> 00:29:25,139
Alfresco.

537
00:29:25,163 --> 00:29:28,443
Alfresco. A hot dog in the park.

538
00:29:28,467 --> 00:29:30,211
I must remember that.

539
00:29:30,235 --> 00:29:33,414
Well, poor Frank Larson.

540
00:29:33,438 --> 00:29:36,150
Doesn't know what
he's missing, does he?

541
00:29:36,174 --> 00:29:40,822
You know, if he was
a creature of his habit,

542
00:29:40,846 --> 00:29:42,290
he must have been in the process

543
00:29:42,314 --> 00:29:44,058
of changing partners
when he was murdered.

544
00:29:44,082 --> 00:29:45,226
Yeah.

545
00:29:45,250 --> 00:29:47,362
He dumped Andrea.

546
00:29:47,386 --> 00:29:49,397
Think maybe she's
a very poor loser?

547
00:29:49,421 --> 00:29:52,900
Maybe.

548
00:29:52,924 --> 00:29:57,294
And... maybe her
mother's covering for her.

549
00:30:00,132 --> 00:30:03,378
You think maybe Phyllis
found out what was going on?

550
00:30:03,402 --> 00:30:06,981
Was outraged and
maybe did it herself?

551
00:30:07,005 --> 00:30:09,384
Well, that, too. That, too.

552
00:30:09,408 --> 00:30:12,537
There's only one
way to find out.

553
00:30:19,117 --> 00:30:20,847
What's everybody
looking at us for?

554
00:30:23,622 --> 00:30:25,066
I have no idea.

555
00:30:25,090 --> 00:30:27,735
Want another?

556
00:30:27,759 --> 00:30:29,785
No, thanks.

557
00:30:33,265 --> 00:30:35,257
Garçon.

558
00:30:39,037 --> 00:30:40,782
- Oh.
- Hi.

559
00:30:40,806 --> 00:30:42,150
You got a minute to talk?

560
00:30:42,174 --> 00:30:45,787
I've got all the
time in the world.

561
00:30:45,811 --> 00:30:47,689
Is Andrea at home?

562
00:30:47,713 --> 00:30:49,223
Uh, no. She's in class.

563
00:30:49,247 --> 00:30:51,059
Oh, she was thrilled with
that book you gave her,

564
00:30:51,083 --> 00:30:52,460
by the way.

565
00:30:52,484 --> 00:30:54,195
Books are such
treasures for her.

566
00:30:54,219 --> 00:30:55,396
Good.

567
00:30:55,420 --> 00:30:56,900
She's always been
such a good student.

568
00:30:57,856 --> 00:31:00,917
I-I imagine she has
lots of boyfriends.

569
00:31:01,993 --> 00:31:04,172
Not really.

570
00:31:04,196 --> 00:31:05,707
Getting an education

571
00:31:05,731 --> 00:31:09,224
is the most important
thing in her life right now.

572
00:31:13,872 --> 00:31:18,219
You must know the
most important thing

573
00:31:18,243 --> 00:31:21,823
is for the client to
be completely honest

574
00:31:21,847 --> 00:31:23,491
with her lawyer.

575
00:31:23,515 --> 00:31:26,027
You don't think
I'm being honest?

576
00:31:26,051 --> 00:31:28,029
I don't think you're
telling me everything.

577
00:31:28,053 --> 00:31:30,284
Of course I am.

578
00:31:31,890 --> 00:31:35,349
Well, what about Andrea's
affair with Frank Larson?

579
00:31:42,267 --> 00:31:43,478
That's none of your business.

580
00:31:43,502 --> 00:31:45,947
None of my business?

581
00:31:45,971 --> 00:31:49,250
And it's got nothing
to do with my case.

582
00:31:49,274 --> 00:31:51,219
The D.A. won't
look at it that way.

583
00:31:51,243 --> 00:31:53,921
The D.A. doesn't know.

584
00:31:53,945 --> 00:31:58,526
Phyllis, if I found
out about it,

585
00:31:58,550 --> 00:32:01,229
it's likely she'll
find out about it.

586
00:32:01,253 --> 00:32:03,531
And-And I have
to defuse this thing

587
00:32:03,555 --> 00:32:05,500
before she takes it
into the courtroom

588
00:32:05,524 --> 00:32:07,584
and uses it to blow
us out of the water.

589
00:32:12,464 --> 00:32:15,843
No. No.

590
00:32:15,867 --> 00:32:19,447
I absolutely forbid you
to bring this up in court.

591
00:32:19,471 --> 00:32:21,215
It's an embarrassment.

592
00:32:21,239 --> 00:32:23,217
It would ruin my
daughter's career

593
00:32:23,241 --> 00:32:25,506
before it's even begun.

594
00:32:29,648 --> 00:32:33,983
Your word, Mr. Matlock,
or I get a new lawyer.

595
00:32:39,458 --> 00:32:42,670
Miss Richards, you
work for the law firm

596
00:32:42,694 --> 00:32:44,205
of McPherson, Steele,
Larson and Marcus,

597
00:32:44,229 --> 00:32:45,540
is that correct?

598
00:32:45,564 --> 00:32:47,775
Yes, it is. I've been
there almost six years.

599
00:32:47,799 --> 00:32:51,446
And on the day Frank
Larson was murdered,

600
00:32:51,470 --> 00:32:53,681
did you witness an argument
between him and the defendant,

601
00:32:53,705 --> 00:32:55,183
Phyllis Todd?

602
00:32:55,207 --> 00:33:00,188
Well, yes, but it was more
of a misunderstanding, really.

603
00:33:00,212 --> 00:33:01,689
And... and it wasn't a big deal,

604
00:33:01,713 --> 00:33:02,857
because it
happened all the time.

605
00:33:02,881 --> 00:33:03,991
I see.

606
00:33:04,015 --> 00:33:06,861
Did they argue frequently?

607
00:33:06,885 --> 00:33:10,798
Well, Mr. Larson was a
very difficult man to work for.

608
00:33:10,822 --> 00:33:12,518
Just answer the
question, please.

609
00:33:13,658 --> 00:33:14,936
Yes, they did.

610
00:33:14,960 --> 00:33:16,137
Would you say that

611
00:33:16,161 --> 00:33:19,340
this particular argument
upset the defendant?

612
00:33:19,364 --> 00:33:20,808
Phyllis is very sensitive.

613
00:33:20,832 --> 00:33:23,344
They always upset her.

614
00:33:23,368 --> 00:33:26,547
And when you
tried to console her,

615
00:33:26,571 --> 00:33:29,268
what did she say to you?

616
00:33:31,109 --> 00:33:33,221
Well, I can't remember
her exact words.

617
00:33:33,245 --> 00:33:35,942
Your own words
will be just fine.

618
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:40,428
Uh, she didn't mean it.

619
00:33:40,452 --> 00:33:42,250
What did she say?

620
00:33:46,157 --> 00:33:48,970
She said, "I know it's
a terrible thing to think,

621
00:33:48,994 --> 00:33:50,929
but sometimes I
wish he were dead."

622
00:33:54,099 --> 00:33:55,829
Thank you, Miss Richards.

623
00:34:03,775 --> 00:34:06,220
Ah!

624
00:34:06,244 --> 00:34:07,622
Oh, hi!

625
00:34:07,646 --> 00:34:09,156
Mom told me you know

626
00:34:09,180 --> 00:34:10,858
about the affair
I had with Frank.

627
00:34:10,882 --> 00:34:14,795
I'm so ashamed, Mr. Matlock,

628
00:34:14,819 --> 00:34:17,932
and so grateful that you're
not gonna make it public.

629
00:34:17,956 --> 00:34:19,367
Well, your mother told me

630
00:34:19,391 --> 00:34:21,068
she'd fire me if I did.

631
00:34:21,092 --> 00:34:24,472
She needs you
desperately, Mr. Matlock.

632
00:34:24,496 --> 00:34:28,543
And I'm gonna stand
by her as are you.

633
00:34:28,567 --> 00:34:30,278
You're pretty close to
your mother, aren't you?

634
00:34:30,302 --> 00:34:33,080
I love her very much.

635
00:34:33,104 --> 00:34:34,882
She told me that your father

636
00:34:34,906 --> 00:34:37,585
ran off when you
were a little girl.

637
00:34:37,609 --> 00:34:39,754
Must have been very difficult
for your mother and you.

638
00:34:39,778 --> 00:34:42,256
We had each other.

639
00:34:42,280 --> 00:34:45,960
You've made her very proud.

640
00:34:45,984 --> 00:34:49,964
You're almost a lawyer,
and you did it all yourself.

641
00:34:49,988 --> 00:34:52,867
You don't owe anybody
anything except her.

642
00:34:52,891 --> 00:34:56,337
Ben... Oh, excuse me, but
I just overheard something

643
00:34:56,361 --> 00:34:58,372
I think you ought to know about.

644
00:34:58,396 --> 00:34:59,407
Excuse me.

645
00:34:59,431 --> 00:35:00,708
Sure.

646
00:35:00,732 --> 00:35:04,011
I just heard Julie tell
Lieutenant Brooks to step up

647
00:35:04,035 --> 00:35:05,580
his search for the
murder weapon.

648
00:35:05,604 --> 00:35:07,548
Says if and when
the police find it,

649
00:35:07,572 --> 00:35:09,750
she can wrap this
case up in a day.

650
00:35:09,774 --> 00:35:10,985
She has a new lead.

651
00:35:11,009 --> 00:35:13,478
Sounds like it.

652
00:35:49,014 --> 00:35:50,949
You better be careful.

653
00:35:56,221 --> 00:35:58,816
You might break a
fingernail or something.

654
00:36:02,427 --> 00:36:04,772
Could you help me?
I'm looking for something

655
00:36:04,796 --> 00:36:07,408
that got thrown out
in here by mistake.

656
00:36:07,432 --> 00:36:08,957
I'll pay you.

657
00:36:17,142 --> 00:36:19,253
What's it look like?

658
00:36:19,277 --> 00:36:21,922
It's a, it's a marble bookend.

659
00:36:21,946 --> 00:36:23,290
It's about this big.

660
00:36:23,314 --> 00:36:25,626
It's got a flat bottom
and an angular top.

661
00:36:25,650 --> 00:36:28,085
It's, it's really heavy.

662
00:36:31,723 --> 00:36:32,767
Where are you going?!

663
00:36:32,791 --> 00:36:34,191
I just gave you $20!

664
00:36:44,869 --> 00:36:46,714
It'll cost you $50.

665
00:36:46,738 --> 00:36:48,449
$50 more?

666
00:36:48,473 --> 00:36:49,717
It's a bargain.

667
00:36:49,741 --> 00:36:53,906
In another ten seconds
it'll cost you $60 more.

668
00:36:55,046 --> 00:36:57,140
All right.

669
00:37:10,195 --> 00:37:12,840
Nice doing business
with you, sweetie.

670
00:37:12,864 --> 00:37:13,888
Eh.

671
00:37:33,852 --> 00:37:37,698
If Ben could see you like
this, he'd go for you in a minute.

672
00:37:37,722 --> 00:37:39,667
Just drive.

673
00:37:39,691 --> 00:37:41,402
How'd it go?

674
00:37:41,426 --> 00:37:43,604
She bought it... literally.

675
00:37:43,628 --> 00:37:47,908
Went for the whole thing?

676
00:37:47,932 --> 00:37:49,643
I got 70 bucks out of her.

677
00:37:49,667 --> 00:37:53,781
I don't think she believed
for a second that it was a fake.

678
00:37:53,805 --> 00:37:56,817
Looks like we found
the killer, Conrad.

679
00:37:56,841 --> 00:37:58,385
Looks like.

680
00:38:18,096 --> 00:38:19,640
You ready?

681
00:38:19,664 --> 00:38:21,242
I think so.

682
00:38:21,266 --> 00:38:22,843
I'm a little nervous.

683
00:38:22,867 --> 00:38:25,212
Well, I won't be asking you

684
00:38:25,236 --> 00:38:29,984
any questions about you
and, you know, Frank Larson.

685
00:38:30,008 --> 00:38:31,919
Look at it this way...

686
00:38:31,943 --> 00:38:34,622
As a student of law, it
might be good for you

687
00:38:34,646 --> 00:38:36,724
to see how it feels
up on the stand, right?

688
00:38:36,748 --> 00:38:39,794
- Right.
- Right.

689
00:38:39,818 --> 00:38:43,998
You know, your
mother still thinks

690
00:38:44,022 --> 00:38:46,801
you're the sweet young girl
that she devoted her life to

691
00:38:46,825 --> 00:38:48,536
after your father left.

692
00:38:48,560 --> 00:38:50,304
You make it sound
like you don't.

693
00:38:50,328 --> 00:38:54,742
Well, it's hard to imagine
a sweet young girl

694
00:38:54,766 --> 00:38:57,378
falling for somebody
like Frank Larson.

695
00:38:57,402 --> 00:39:00,748
Could be you wanted
something else.

696
00:39:00,772 --> 00:39:02,283
Like what?

697
00:39:02,307 --> 00:39:05,019
Maybe a job.

698
00:39:05,043 --> 00:39:07,354
You're wrong about
me, Mr. Matlock.

699
00:39:07,378 --> 00:39:08,689
I hope so.

700
00:39:08,713 --> 00:39:11,525
We have a big problem, Andrea.

701
00:39:11,549 --> 00:39:15,596
I don't think your mother
has much of a chance in there.

702
00:39:15,620 --> 00:39:18,499
You think they'll
find Mom guilty?

703
00:39:18,523 --> 00:39:20,568
I've done my best,
but I have to be honest.

704
00:39:20,592 --> 00:39:21,936
It doesn't look good.

705
00:39:21,960 --> 00:39:26,106
But she's innocent, and
she said you were the best.

706
00:39:26,130 --> 00:39:28,309
She said that you
would get her off!

707
00:39:28,333 --> 00:39:32,780
Well, there seems to be
a big difference these days

708
00:39:32,804 --> 00:39:36,383
between the way your
mother sees things

709
00:39:36,407 --> 00:39:38,467
and the way they really are.

710
00:39:50,755 --> 00:39:55,669
You, you are the, uh, uh,
daughter of the defendant.

711
00:39:55,693 --> 00:39:56,971
Is that correct?

712
00:39:56,995 --> 00:39:58,105
Yes, I am.

713
00:39:58,129 --> 00:40:01,208
And you live together
in the same house?

714
00:40:01,232 --> 00:40:02,643
Yes.

715
00:40:02,667 --> 00:40:08,382
Did your mother ever talk to you
about her employer Frank Larson?

716
00:40:08,406 --> 00:40:10,050
Yes.

717
00:40:10,074 --> 00:40:12,119
Di-Did you get the impression

718
00:40:12,143 --> 00:40:14,889
that she enjoyed
working for him?

719
00:40:14,913 --> 00:40:18,692
He paid her well,
but I don't think

720
00:40:18,716 --> 00:40:20,861
she actually enjoyed
working for him, no.

721
00:40:20,885 --> 00:40:22,029
Why not?

722
00:40:22,053 --> 00:40:23,697
He was very
unpleasant and unfair.

723
00:40:23,721 --> 00:40:27,334
He used to blame her for
things that were his fault

724
00:40:27,358 --> 00:40:29,703
and he used to belittle her

725
00:40:29,727 --> 00:40:31,238
in front of everybody
at the office.

726
00:40:31,262 --> 00:40:34,308
How long was he unfair to her?

727
00:40:34,332 --> 00:40:36,744
For as long as I can remember.

728
00:40:36,768 --> 00:40:39,480
So arguments with her

729
00:40:39,504 --> 00:40:42,816
were a fairly common
everyday occurrence?

730
00:40:42,840 --> 00:40:44,451
Yes.

731
00:40:44,475 --> 00:40:46,520
When you came home
the night of the murder,

732
00:40:46,544 --> 00:40:50,257
did your mother appear
to be angry or distraught?

733
00:40:50,281 --> 00:40:51,458
No.

734
00:40:51,482 --> 00:40:55,396
Did she tell you of the
most recent argument

735
00:40:55,420 --> 00:40:56,830
he had had with her?

736
00:40:56,854 --> 00:40:59,199
All she said was that
she needed to talk to him,

737
00:40:59,223 --> 00:41:01,335
which was why she
went back that night.

738
00:41:01,359 --> 00:41:03,570
She knew he'd be working late.

739
00:41:03,594 --> 00:41:05,673
Uh-huh. And she left
the house at what time?

740
00:41:05,697 --> 00:41:09,343
Oh, 7:15, 7:30,
something like that.

741
00:41:09,367 --> 00:41:12,880
And what, what, what did
you do while she was gone?

742
00:41:12,904 --> 00:41:15,382
I studied, and
then I went to bed.

743
00:41:15,406 --> 00:41:17,451
Well, uh, did, uh...

744
00:41:17,475 --> 00:41:20,254
You-you didn't have,
you didn't have dinner?

745
00:41:20,278 --> 00:41:22,713
I think I ate on
campus that night.

746
00:41:25,650 --> 00:41:27,227
Oh, I remember now.

747
00:41:27,251 --> 00:41:29,596
I, um, I ordered a pizza.

748
00:41:29,620 --> 00:41:32,933
Oh.

749
00:41:32,957 --> 00:41:37,304
What time did the,
uh, did the pizza arrive?

750
00:41:37,328 --> 00:41:40,607
Um, just before 8:00, I guess.

751
00:41:40,631 --> 00:41:43,210
Uh-huh, was your
mother home by then?

752
00:41:43,234 --> 00:41:46,580
No, she didn't get home
until after I'd gone to bed

753
00:41:46,604 --> 00:41:49,350
sometime around 9:00 or so.

754
00:41:49,374 --> 00:41:52,619
So, you, you accepted
delivery of the pizza.

755
00:41:52,643 --> 00:41:54,888
Yes.

756
00:41:54,912 --> 00:41:57,541
Okay. Mr. Matthews.

757
00:42:00,852 --> 00:42:03,864
Do you recognize this man?

758
00:42:03,888 --> 00:42:05,866
No.

759
00:42:05,890 --> 00:42:09,770
Your Honor, I have to
confer with my attorney.

760
00:42:09,794 --> 00:42:11,538
You're out of order.

761
00:42:11,562 --> 00:42:13,073
Please sit down.

762
00:42:13,097 --> 00:42:14,975
I must talk to you.

763
00:42:14,999 --> 00:42:17,244
I agree with you, Your
Honor. She's out of order.

764
00:42:17,268 --> 00:42:18,445
You can't do this.

765
00:42:18,469 --> 00:42:20,214
You're out of order!

766
00:42:20,238 --> 00:42:21,715
You can't!

767
00:42:21,739 --> 00:42:24,368
Now sit down or I'll
have you removed.

768
00:42:40,091 --> 00:42:41,957
You don't recognize this man?

769
00:42:43,394 --> 00:42:45,886
No.

770
00:42:47,765 --> 00:42:52,980
Well, the fact is he doesn't
recognize you either.

771
00:42:53,004 --> 00:42:57,084
He's the man who
delivered the pizza

772
00:42:57,108 --> 00:42:59,720
to your house that night,

773
00:42:59,744 --> 00:43:01,889
and he says the door was opened

774
00:43:01,913 --> 00:43:04,892
not by you, but by
her, your mother.

775
00:43:04,916 --> 00:43:07,194
Objection! Mr. Matlock
can't testify for the witness!

776
00:43:07,218 --> 00:43:08,762
He saw her because
she was at home.

777
00:43:08,786 --> 00:43:10,898
And your mother didn't use
that card to enter the building

778
00:43:10,922 --> 00:43:12,466
that night, did she?

779
00:43:12,490 --> 00:43:13,734
Stop it!

780
00:43:13,758 --> 00:43:14,735
Madam!

781
00:43:14,759 --> 00:43:16,470
You did, didn't you?!

782
00:43:16,494 --> 00:43:17,905
I'm changing my plea!

783
00:43:17,929 --> 00:43:19,173
I'm guilty!

784
00:43:19,197 --> 00:43:20,574
Bailiff, restrain this woman.

785
00:43:20,598 --> 00:43:23,277
Andrea, who killed Frank Larson?

786
00:43:23,301 --> 00:43:24,311
I did!

787
00:43:24,335 --> 00:43:25,412
I killed him!

788
00:43:25,436 --> 00:43:27,081
You must stop him, Judge!

789
00:43:27,105 --> 00:43:28,615
Bailiff, remove the defendant!

790
00:43:28,639 --> 00:43:29,783
No!

791
00:43:29,807 --> 00:43:31,935
Don't.

792
00:43:38,549 --> 00:43:43,544
Who killed Frank Larson?

793
00:43:47,692 --> 00:43:50,003
I killed him.

794
00:43:50,027 --> 00:43:52,895
No.

795
00:44:01,873 --> 00:44:04,785
I was having an affair with him.

796
00:44:04,809 --> 00:44:06,787
Andrea, please.

797
00:44:06,811 --> 00:44:10,457
He broke it off.

798
00:44:10,481 --> 00:44:13,760
I was so stunned and so furious.

799
00:44:13,784 --> 00:44:16,811
